From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Anonymous Submission #60 <anon@example.invalid>
Date: Fri, 28 Aug 2026 12:56:41 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] RNGuard: x86-64 backend using memory protection keys

Implements the RNGuard gate on x86-64 with Intel MPK. Pages in the
.rnguard_iso section are mapped user-accessible (U/S=1) and assigned
protection key 1, so an ordinary kernel access is refused twice over: by
SMAP because the page is user-accessible, and by PKRU because the key
denies access. enable_rnguard_iso_access() opens both for the duration of a
gate window; disable_rnguard_iso_access() closes them again. The pair is
refcounted so nesting is safe.

The protected per-CPU pseudo-stack is a page-aligned object in the same
section, with an unmapped guard page placed first so a downward overflow
faults instead of corrupting the neighbouring crngs and batched_entropy
pools. Installing the guard page must happen after the section's 4K split,
or lookup_address() returns a PMD and clearing _PAGE_PRESENT would unmap
2 MiB of the direct map; the PG_LEVEL_4K check enforces that ordering.

The stack switch reuses upstream's call_on_stack(), which is already
objtool-annotated and stores the caller's RSP at the top of the new stack.
The unwinder is taught about the new stack type so a backtrace taken while
the RNG is running on it terminates cleanly rather than walking off into
unrelated memory. Stack bounds are computed arithmetically from the static
per-CPU base, never by dereferencing protected memory, so the unwinder
stays safe to call with the gate closed and from noinstr context.

Note that an exception taken while the stack pointer is on the pseudo-stack
is unrecoverable: exception entry re-arms SMAP and then keeps pushing onto
the protected stack. No WARN, BUG_ON or other trap-capable code may appear
below a switch point.
